A||||8||Greg Piet||Running in diagnostic mode only
Z||000000||Greg Piet||08-23-99||11:44 PM||||I replaced the distributor and set the timing.  The car was peachy for a few days, then it died in the middle of the road with NO ELECTRICAL POWER AT ALL.  I realize that I need to replace the battery cables, but with its present state I dont see why the battery cables would cause it to behave in this fashion.<br>After scratching my head I took the battery connections off and put them back on.<br>Unfortunately the car would now not run except in diagnostic mode.<br>I tried taking the vaccum hose off of the MAP sensor, and it seemed to run about 1/2 a second longer (on chryslers it would run in "creep along mode"), or on rare occasion chug at idle poorly for a while.  I am rather confused since it runs at about 1500-2000 RPM in diag mode at the proper timing setting, and as soon as i yank the wire to disable diag mode it dies.  I got it to run w/o diag mode, by getting someone to fan the gas while i retard the timing about 30 more degrees (it seems).  Then it runs OK, but it wont start becasue it the timing is screwy now(40 deg btc).  I disconnected the TPC and the computer successfully found that problem, but I am HOPING it is some other sensor that I am unaware of, or that it is common that bad battery cables will do this. I am THINKING it is the ECM, but I dont want to believe it.<p>If you know the solution to this problem, please help me, <br>(Email   gregpiet@physics.unlv.edu)<br> for it is driving me nuts!<br>(and for all of you who are shaking their finger at me for not replacing the battery cables, I didnt know the length, so I pulled them out today and I will be replacing them in the morning!)<br>
Z||000001||DJRice||08-24-99||08:08 AM||||Do you have no electrical power at all?  Or do you just have no spark?  If you just replaced your distributor, Id say you need to look there for your problem.  Could be the Coil depending on the car.  4/6 cyl? The computer is probably not the problem.<p>Definitely replace the battery cables.  There  is one medium sized red wire that hooks to a distribution block under the main engine harness connector.  (If this is anything but an '84)  That red wire feeds all the electronics in the car, if that is loose or corroded, you will have a completely dead car.  There are also several ground locations, so check all of the ground straps to make sure things are grounded good.
Z||000002||Greg Piet||08-24-99||10:16 PM||||I now have power to the car.  It temporarily died (I think due to a bad aux ground wire from the battery<br>I replaced the battery cables, and the coolant sensor (it was out of spec for its resistance)<br>Right now It will run at idle in diagnostic mode (approx 1500 rpm), but in normal mode it fluctuates from 500-1250 rpm at about 1 cycle per second. I appear to be getting spark (since it runs in diag mode, and it tried to run in normal mode)<br>I am not getting any error codes.<br>When i disconnected the TPS, it gave me an error code, so I am assuming that the TPS is OK.<br>I have compared the resistance of the IAC valve with a brand new one at checker auto<br>mine 55 ohms (in car) theirs 46 ohms (out of the box)<br>Battery cables have been replaced, and nothing new.<p>I am wondering about the computer (running out of options), or possible fusable links, but they are sealed (right).  I found 2 by the battery (are there more).  Another thing is the new distrubutor looks like the old one , but it came with an igntion module(possible problem?) <br>I havent looked at the egr valve, but I thought the car would still run with the egr valve screwy, just not with any pep.<br>I am thinking that the only options left are to replace the IAC valve or to replace the computer, but first check the 2 fusable links (which I dont see to be a problem because the  ECM gives codes, and it runs in diag mode.<br>Any continued help with this would be greatly appreciated, and I will be sure to post the solution after it is resolved.
Z||000003||DJRice||08-25-99||07:08 AM||||Your fusible links are probably ok, because the engine does run.  My next step would be to replace the IAC.  If you didnt take it out to examine it, you have no idea what condition the business end of it is in.  It could have some deposits on it that prevent it from doing its job.  My car fluctuates at idle when warming up, and replacing the IAC was the suggestion I was given.<p>The problem is probably not the computer.  It is able to check itself and if there was a problem you would get a code 54 or 55 I believe.<p>Good luck getting her back on the road
Z||000004||Greg Piet||08-25-99||01:39 PM||||I took the IAC off last night and it was DIRTY!<br>I cleaned it off and cleasen out the port, but to no avail<br>I was thinking the oxygen sensor has no way of testing, but even if it was shot wouldnt the car run?<br>I would test the TPS, because i think it is just a potentiometer, so by checking the resistance i should be able to tell wha the problem is.<p>WILL THE CAR RUN IF THE EGR SOLENOID IS  SHOT?
Z||000005||Greg Piet||08-26-99||01:14 PM||||I replaced the IAC valve, and it is still being a pain.<br>I got it to roughly idle for about a minute and it gave me a 3-5  now i know the IAC is ok for i just replaced it and i cleaned out its mating surface in the manifold.  I suppose this is likely a vaccum leak then?
Z||000006||theogre||08-26-99||01:57 PM||||make sure there are no vac leaks. Carefully inspect All the hardlines and connectors. <p>You replaced the IAC? You did make sure the IAC pintle was in far enough before you installed it? If it wasn't you may have it jammed against the seat.<p>After replacement the IAC needs to recalibrate. you have to get the motor over 1500 RPM and hold it there for at least 60 seconds. (It takes 30 before the ECM even thinks about recallibrating the IAC.) The ECM will close the IAC to the seat, and then count out X steps... It will then use that for a baseline. (IAC recalls each time the car is run and it hits 1500+ for the first time and stays there long enough.)<p>Clear all the codes, and try that. Your IAC may not be close to calibrated at all since you just replaced it. It might need a couple tries.<p>If you still keep getting IAC codes, either the new one is bad, there is a wiring problem, or the ECM has a problem. There's nothing between the ECM and the IAC but wire and connectors. 
Z||000007||DJRice||08-26-99||02:44 PM||||Ogre, I did a tune up on my 87 V6, but I didnt get around to cleaning the IAC.  I am hoping that when I do it will solve my rough idle when I start the car in the morning.  Whats the procedure for making sure it is set  correctly ("pintle is far enough in") before I reinstall it?<p>Thanks in advance for the wisdom...... 
Z||000008||Greg Piet||08-26-99||10:04 PM||||OK it is fixed!<br>Apparently the magnetic ring on the new/remanufactured distrubutor (FROM CHECKER AUTO) was elevated, and that caused intermittant spark which for some odd reason let it run in diagnostic mode, but not in normal mode.  The really odd thig is that it worked for a few 100 miles then kaput!<p>By taking the distributor out AGAIN, i noticed there was a ring which resembled a castle nut which then set into the distrubutor gear.  This had slop on it so i banged the magnetic ring on top to secure the  "castle nut" into the gear.<br>I then timed it and yanked the diag wire...<br>voila! it is a happy fiero again.<p>Thank you all for your help/suggestions, and now that I am aware of this site I will try to visit regularly and assist others if I can<p><br>
